<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-function-notation" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Function Notation</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Compute the value of a function in different situations
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Guidance on the Opening Activity</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    10 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To introduce the idea of a function through a familiar real-world context before any formal notation is shown. Learners build their own mapping using food and prices, which makes the input-output idea concrete and personal before it is written using symbols.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Walk around and check that each learner's arrow diagram assigns exactly one price to each food item. Where a learner has assigned two different prices to the same item, ask them whether that would make sense in a real shop. Use their responses to lead naturally into the idea that a function gives exactly one output for each input. Hold off on introducing <m>f(x)</m> notation until after the class has discussed the arrow diagrams together.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <activity xml:id="activity-discovering-function-rule-1">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Imagine you are setting the prices for items sold at a school canteen shop. Each food item must have exactly one price. In this activity, you will create your own rule connecting food items to their prices, and then learn how mathematicians write that kind of rule using a special notation.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Choose four food items you would sell at a canteen shop and decide on a price for each one. Draw an arrow diagram where the left bubble contains your food items (the inputs) and the right bubble contains their prices in shillings (the outputs). Draw an arrow from each food item to its price.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Here is an example to get you started.
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-food-arrow-diagram">
                    <caption>An arrow diagram showing food items mapped to their prices.</caption>
                    <image xml:id="img-food-arrow-diagram" width="65%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, font=\sffamily]

                                % Left bubble (inputs)
                                \draw[thick, blue!60!black, fill=blue!8]
                                    (-3.2, 0) ellipse (2cm and 3cm);
                                \node[font=\bfseries, blue!60!black] at (-3.2, 3.4) {Food Item};

                                % Right bubble (outputs)
                                \draw[thick, orange!70!black, fill=orange!8]
                                    (3.2, 0) ellipse (2cm and 3cm);
                                \node[font=\bfseries, orange!70!black] at (3.2, 3.4) {Price (Ksh)};

                                % Input labels
                                \node at (-3.2,  1.6) {Mandazi};
                                \node at (-3.2,  0.5) {Juice};
                                \node at (-3.2, -0.6) {Samosa};
                                \node at (-3.2, -1.7) {Banana};

                                % Output labels
                                \node at (3.2,  1.6) {10};
                                \node at (3.2,  0.5) {30};
                                \node at (3.2, -0.6) {20};
                                \node at (3.2, -1.7) {5};

                                % Arrows
                                \draw[->, thick, gray!70] (-2.1, 1.6)  -- (2.1, 1.6);
                                \draw[->, thick, gray!70] (-2.1, 0.5)  -- (2.1, 0.5);
                                \draw[->, thick, gray!70] (-2.1, -0.6) -- (2.1, -0.6);
                                \draw[->, thick, gray!70] (-2.1, -1.7) -- (2.1, -1.7);

                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Look at your arrow diagram. How many arrows leave each food item? What would it mean if one food item had two different arrows pointing to two different prices? Discuss this with a partner.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now suppose you name your pricing rule <m>f</m>. Instead of drawing an arrow every time, mathematicians write <m>f(\text{food item})</m> to mean "the price of that food item." For example, using the diagram above, <m>f(\text{Mandazi}) = 10</m>. Write three more statements like this using your own arrow diagram.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now replace the food items with numbers. Suppose the inputs are <m>x = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4</m> and the rule is "multiply the input by 2 and add 1." To avoid confusion, we'll call this <m>g(x)</m>. Complete the arrow diagram and then fill in the table below.
                </p>
                <tabular top="minor" bottom="minor">
                    <row>
                        <cell><alert>Input <m>x</m></alert></cell>
                        <cell><m>0</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>2</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>3</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>4</m></cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><alert>Output <m>g(x)</m></alert></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{0}</m></cell>
                        <cell><m>\phantom{0}</m></cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Write the rule from (d) using function notation. Your answer should start with <m>g(x) = \ldots</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use your rule to find the value of <m>g(50)</m> and <m>g(88)</m>. Share your working with a classmate and check whether you agree.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>
                    15 minutes
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To formalise what learners discovered in the activity by introducing the definition of a function, the terms domain and range, and the proper use of <m>f(x)</m> notation.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work:</alert> Link the food-price arrow diagram directly to the definition. The food items were the domain (the allowed inputs) and the prices were the range (the actual outputs produced). Use a learner's own diagram on the board to show this before writing any symbols.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Introducing f(x):</alert> Make clear that <m>f(x)</m> is simply a short way of writing "the output when the input is <m>x</m>." It is not <m>f</m> multiplied by <m>x</m>. This is a common misconception to address early and directly.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Domain and Range: </alert>  Keep the language simple. The domain is all the inputs the function is allowed to receive, and the range is all the output values it actually produces. Avoid introducing co-domain at this level, as it is not required for Grade 11.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->
    <definition xml:id="def-function-notation">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A <term>function</term> is a rule that connects each input value to exactly one output value. We give the function a name, usually a letter such as <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, or <m>h</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                The complete collection of values that are allowed as inputs is called the <term>domain</term> of the function. The collection of all output values that the function actually produces is called the <term>range</term> of the function.
            </p>
            <p>
                When we want to write the output that corresponds to a particular input <m>x</m>, we write <m>f(x)</m>, which is read as "<m>f</m> of <m>x</m>." This is called the <term>function value</term> at <m>x</m>, or simply the output at <m>x</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Because a function produces exactly one output for each input, the same input value can never give two different outputs. However, two different input values are allowed to produce the same output.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <insight>
        <p>
            We can think of a function as a machine. An input value goes in, the rule inside the machine performs an operation, and a single output value comes out.
        </p>
        <figure xml:id="fig-function-machine-notation">
            <caption>The input-output process of a function.</caption>
            <image xml:id="img-function-machine" width="60%">
                <latex-image>
                    \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, font=\sffamily]

                        % Input arrow
                        \draw[->, ultra thick, blue!80!black] (-4,0) -- (-1.8,0);
                        \node[align=center] at (-3,0.9)
                        {\textbf{Input}\\$x$};

                        % Function box
                        \draw[thick, rounded corners=10pt, fill=blue!10]
                        (-1.8,-1.5) rectangle (1.8,1.5);

                        \node[align=center] at (0,0)
                        {\textbf{Function}\\$f$};

                        % Output arrow
                        \draw[->, ultra thick, orange!80!black] (1.8,0) -- (4,0);

                        \node[align=center] at (3,0.9)
                        {\textbf{Output}\\$f(x)$};

                    \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
        </figure>
        <p>
            To find the output for a given input, we replace the variable in the rule with that input value and then simplify. This process is called <term>evaluating</term> the function.
        </p>
    </insight>

    <exploration>
        <title>The Function Machine</title>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-function-machine" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="4:3" source="jsxgraph/functions/function-machine-evaluate.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsxgraph-function-machine" surface="jsxboard" aspect="4:3"/>
            <instructions>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Drag the slider to set <m>x = 3</m>, then click <c>Evaluate f(x)</c>. Watch the input travel into the machine and the output travel out. Does your answer match Example 1 in this section?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Set the slider to a negative value, such as <m>x = -2</m>, and evaluate again. What substitution step appears in the middle of the animation, and what does it represent?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Predict the output for <m>x = 0</m> before clicking <c>Evaluate f(x)</c>. Were you correct?
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Try two different input values that give the same output. Is this possible for this machine? Explain why or why not, using what you learned about domain and range.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </instructions>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-positive">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given the function <m>f(x)=4x-7</m>, determine <m>f(3)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Replace every occurrence of <m>x</m> with <m>3</m> and simplify.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> f(3) \amp = 4(3) - 7</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 12 - 7</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 5</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the output of the function when the input is <m>3</m> is equal to <m>5</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-negative">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Given <m>g(t)=t^2+2t</m>, evaluate <m>g(-4)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Replace every occurrence of <m>t</m> with <m>-4</m> and simplify.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> g(-4) \amp =(-4)^2+2(-4)</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp =16-8</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp =8 </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, <m>g(-4)=8</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-eval-expression">
        <statement>
            <p>
                If <m>h(x)=5x+1</m>, determine <m>h(a+3)</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The input here is the expression <m>a+3</m> rather than a single number. Replace every occurrence of <m>x</m> with <m>a+3</m> and expand.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> h(a+3) \amp = 5(a+3)+1</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 5a+15+1</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 5a+16</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, <m>h(a+3)=5a+16</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-function-notation-word">
        <statement>
            <p>
                A taxi company charges a fixed fee of <m>200</m> shillings and an additional 
                <m>50</m> shillings per kilometre travelled. The total fare is given by
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>F(k)=50k+200</md>
            </p>

            <p>
                 where <m>k</m> represents the number of kilometres travelled. Find <m>F(6)</m> and explain what your answer means.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Replace <m>k</m> with <m>6</m> and simplify.
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow> F(6) \amp =50(6)+200</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 300 + 200</mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = 500</mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
            <p>
                Therefore, the cost of travelling <m>6</m> kilometres is 
                <m>500</m> shillings.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-function-notation">
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>f(x)=4x-1</m>, determine <m>f(5)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>19</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given <m>g(x)=x^2+2x</m>, evaluate <m>g(-3)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>3</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>h(t)=3t+7</m>. Find the value of <m>h(0)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>7</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>p(x)=\frac{x-4}{2}</m>, determine the value of <m>p(10)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>3</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given <m>m(x)=2x^2-3</m>, evaluate <m>m(-2)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>5</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Let <m>f(x)=5x+2</m>. Write an expression for <m>f(a)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>5a+2</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If <m>g(x)=3x-4</m>, find <m>g(k+2)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>3k+2</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given <m>h(x)=x^2+1</m>, determine <m>h(2a)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>4a^2+1</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A mobile network charges <m>10</m> shillings per minute for a call and a fixed connection fee of <m>5</m> shillings. 
                    The total cost is given by <m>C(t)=10t+5</m>, where <m>t</m> is the number of minutes. Find the value of <m>C(8)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>85</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A farmer sells eggs at <m>15</m> shillings each. If the total amount earned is represented by 
                    <m>E(n)=15n</m>, where <m>n</m> is the number of eggs sold, determine the total amount earned when 24 eggs are sold.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p><m>E(24) = 15\times 24 = 360</m></p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Allow these learners to keep their food-price arrow diagram visible throughout the lesson as a reference. When evaluating a function, encourage them to first write out the step "replace <m>x</m> with ___" in words before substituting numbers. Focus on numeric inputs (Exercises 1 to 5) and introduce algebraic inputs only once the substitution process feels secure.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask these learners to work backwards: given that <m>f(x) = 3x + 1</m> and <m>f(a) = 19</m>, find the value of <m>a</m>. They can also be challenged to create their own real-world function, write it in function notation, and then pose two evaluation questions for a classmate to solve.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>

    <exploration xml:id="explore-function-notation">
        <title>Exploring function notation</title>
        <p>
            Move the slider to change the input <m>x</m>. The point on the curve, together with the values of <m>x</m>, <m>f(x)</m> and the ordered pair <m>(x,\,f(x))</m>, updates immediately, showing that every input has exactly one matching output.
        </p>
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-function-notation" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/functions/function-notation.js">
            <slate xml:id="jsx-function-notation" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
        </interactive>
    </exploration>

